      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hazen was incorporated on July 8, 1884, and named after early settler Dr. William Cogswell Hazen.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Hazen,_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened in Hazen that same year, cementing the town’s emerging identity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Hazen,_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1854 William Cogswell Hazen arrived from Virginia with his family and twenty‑one slaves to start a cotton plantation north of present‑day Hazen.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Hazen,_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Chicago, Rock Island and Pacific Railroad turned Hazen into a vital freight stop, letting farmers ship rice and cotton to Little Rock and Memphis.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Hazen,_Arkansas",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "An F4 tornado struck Hazen on March 21, 1952, killing five residents and demolishing the Center Point Baptist Church.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Hazen,_Arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "City of Hazen prides itself on small‑town values, guiding growth while preserving its historical, cultural, and natural heritage.",
